## 3. Comparison Matrix: Kùzu vs. SQL Ecosystem

| Feature Domain | SQLite | Microsoft SQL Server | Kùzu (KuzuDB) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Architecture | Embedded (in-process) | Client-server (service) | Embedded (in-process) |
| Primary Workload | OLTP / embedded storage | Enterprise OLTP / DW | Graph analytics / RAG / agent memory |
| Data Model | Relational tables + FKs | Relational tables + FKs | Structured property graph |
| Storage Format | Row-oriented B-Tree | Row-store (plus columnstore indexes in some cases) | Columnar + CSR adjacency |
| Query Language | SQL | T-SQL | Cypher |
| Traversal Mechanism | Joins/CTEs | Joins/CTEs | Native variable-length paths |
| Concurrency | Single writer / multi-reader (WAL) | High concurrency (locks/MVCC-like strategies) | Strict single writer / multi-reader |
| Vector Search | Extensions (e.g., sqlite-vss) | Typically external services/tools | Native vector index (HNSW) |

### 3.1 The “Single Writer” Constraint

Kùzu enforces a **strict single-writer model** at the database directory level:

- Only one read-write transaction can be active at a time.
- Multiple readers can query concurrently.

Implications for Python applications:

- Multi-process writers (e.g., `multiprocessing` where multiple processes write to the same DB) will likely fail with locking/transaction errors.
- Recommended pattern: **serialized ingestion** (queue + single writer) + concurrent read-only queries.

